  },
  "grade": "11-12",
  "points": 4,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "Pressing a button increases the number on the screen by the fractional part of that number. For example, if the number on the screen is 1.3, after pressing the button the number on the screen is $1.3 + 0.3 = 1.6$. How many numbers are there that, if the button is pressed less than four times, would result in the number 10 being displayed?",
  "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2023-g1112/q14.webp",
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "3"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "7"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "8"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "12"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "16"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Work backwards",
    "body": "Write a number as $x = n + f$ with integer part $n$ and fractional part $0 \\le f < 1$. Pressing gives $n + 2f$.\n\nWhich $x$ give a number $y = m + g$ ($m$ integer, $0\\le g<1$)? We need $n + 2f = m + g$ with $0 \\le 2f < 2$, so either $n = m$, $f = \\frac g2$, or $n = m - 1$, $f = \\frac{1+g}{2}$. Every number has exactly **two** predecessors.\n\nStarting from 10:\n- 1 press: predecessors of 10 are $10$ and $9.5$.\n- 2 presses: predecessors of these: $10, 9.5$ and $9.25, 8.75$.\n- 3 presses: $10, 9.5, 9.25, 8.75$ and $9.125, 8.625, 8.375, 7.875$.\n\nSince 10 stays 10, every number that reaches 10 in 1 or 2 presses also does so in 3 presses. So the numbers are exactly the 8 numbers in the last list.\n\n**Answer: (C) 8**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "sequences-series",
   "fractions-decimals"
  ],
  "difficulty": 2,
  "duplicateOf": null,
  "reviewNote": null
 },
