  "points": 4,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "What is the smallest positive integer $n$ such that the interval $$\\left[\\frac{n+8}{2}, \\frac{2n+14}{3}\\right]$$ contains at least four natural numbers?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "19"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "18"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "17"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "16"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "none of the previous"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "E",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Try values",
    "body": "The interval has length $\\frac{2n+14}{3} - \\frac{n+8}{2} = \\frac{n+4}{6}$. An interval containing four consecutive integers has length at least 3, so $n + 4 \\ge 18$, $n \\ge 14$.\n\nFor $n = 14$: the interval is $\\left[\\frac{22}{2}, \\frac{42}{3}\\right] = [11, 14]$, which contains $11, 12, 13, 14$ — four natural numbers. So the smallest $n$ is 14, which is not among (A)–(D).\n\n**Answer: (E) none of the previous**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Check below 14",
    "body": "For $n \\le 13$ the length $\\frac{n+4}{6}$ is less than 3, and an interval of length less than 3 contains at most 3 integers. So no $n < 14$ works, and $n = 14$ works as shown. The options 16–19 also work, but they are not the smallest.\n\n**Answer: (E) none of the previous**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
