  "grade": "11-12",
  "points": 5,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "A teacher wishes to divide a group of ten students into pairs for an activity. However, she doesn't want to put Alice and Bill in the same pair, nor does she want Will and Zoe in the same pair. In how many different ways can she make these pairs?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "300"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "600"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "720"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "750"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "945"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Inclusion–exclusion",
    "body": "The number of ways to split $2k$ people into pairs is $(2k-1)(2k-3)\\cdots 1$: the first person picks a partner in $2k-1$ ways, the next unpaired person in $2k-3$ ways, and so on. For 10 people: $9\\cdot7\\cdot5\\cdot3\\cdot1 = 945$.\n\n- Alice with Bill: the other 8 are paired in $7\\cdot5\\cdot3 = 105$ ways.\n- Will with Zoe: also 105.\n- Both: the other 6 in $5\\cdot3 = 15$ ways.\n\nAllowed: $945 - 105 - 105 + 15 = 750$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 750**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Pair Alice first",
    "body": "Alice can be paired with 8 people (not Bill).\n- Alice with Will or Zoe (2 ways): then Will–Zoe can no longer be a pair, and Bill is just one of the 8 remaining people with no restriction: $105$ pairings each → $210$.\n- Alice with one of the other 6 (6 ways): 8 people remain including Will and Zoe, who must not be together: $105 - 15 = 90$ each → $540$.\n\nTotal $210 + 540 = 750$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 750**"
   }
  ],
